  },
];

const goodPoints = [
  {
    label: 'Genuine strength',
    title: 'A real job that does not require a research degree',
    body: 'The medical laboratory science and hospital diagnostics track pays from year one, at a diagnostic chain or hospital lab, with no PhD, no fellowship years, and no competitive postgraduate exam standing between the degree and a paycheck. Most PCB-adjacent science degrees cannot say that.',
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine strength',
    title: 'Demand that does not depend on one employer type',
    body: "Hospitals, standalone diagnostic chains, pharma manufacturers, and contract research organisations all hire biomedical science graduates for overlapping but distinct roles. That employer spread is real insurance against a single sector having a bad hiring year.",
  },
  {
    label: 'Genuine strength',
    title: 'A credible route into research, if you actually want it',
    body: "Unlike a general BSc, a biomedical science degree gives a direct, recognised on-ramp into government and academic research (ICMR, DBT-funded institutes, university labs) through the JRF-SRF ladder, without needing to first bridge through an unrelated life-science master's.",
  },
];

const downsideRows = [
  {
    signal: "You are choosing it thinking it's a shortcut toward becoming a doctor without NEET",
    reality: 'It is not. Biomedical science graduates test samples, run diagnostics, and support research; they do not diagnose or treat patients. That authority sits with an MBBS, and choosing this degree on the assumption that it is "medicine without the exam" sets up a real disappointment two or three years in.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'You expect the degree alone to pay hospital-consultant-level money',
    reality: 'A fresh medical lab technologist typically starts around Rs 1.8-3.5 LPA. That is a real, stable entry job, not a high-income outcome on day one. The income growth in this field comes from stacking accreditation knowledge, a specific test-method specialisation, or lab-management experience on top of the base degree, not from the degree title alone.',
  },
  {
    signal: "You are drawn to the research track because it sounds prestigious, without checking the pay",
    reality: 'JRF and SRF pay is a fixed government fellowship, not a market salary, roughly Rs 37,000 a month rising to roughly Rs 42,000 a month under current UGC/DBT-linked norms. It stays fixed for years regardless of how good your results are, and it only becomes a market-linked scientist salary after clearing a second, separate competitive gate.',
  },
  {
    signal: 'You dislike repetitive, protocol-heavy, quality-controlled work',
    reality: 'Diagnostic lab work runs on strict standard operating procedures and NABL accreditation checks; research work runs on repeated experiments, failed attempts, and detailed lab notebooks. Neither track rewards improvisation. If routine precision genuinely bores you, this degree will feel like a long grind rather than a career.',
  },
];

const payRankedRows = [
  {
    rank: '1',
    track: 'Medical laboratory science and hospital diagnostics',
    pay: 'Rs 1.8-3.5 LPA entry, rising to Rs 3.5-6 LPA as a senior lab scientist and Rs 6-11 LPA as a lab or quality manager at an NABL-accredited facility',
    why: 'Fastest realistic path to real income, and the only track in this degree with a genuine, if slow-building, ownership ceiling above it (see the ownership-path section below).',
  },
  {
    rank: '2',
    track: 'Clinical research and pharma/biotech QC-QA',
    pay: 'Coordinator-level roles typically start in a comparable Rs 2.5-5 LPA band, with a clearer market-driven (not fellowship-fixed) growth curve into project-management and QA-manager pay over 5-8 years',
    why: 'Second on ceiling because pay is negotiated in the open market from year one, not fixed by a fellowship grade, and the skills transfer across employers more easily than lab-bench specialisation does.',
  },
  {
    rank: '3',
    track: 'Biomedical research (government or academic, JRF to scientist)',
    pay: 'A fixed fellowship of roughly Rs 37,000 a month for the first two years, rising to roughly Rs 42,000 a month as SRF from year three, then a government scientist pay scale only after a PhD and a separate competitive selection',
    why: 'Ranked last for realistic near-term ceiling, not because the work is less respectable, but because pay is fellowship-fixed for years and the jump to market-linked scientist pay depends on clearing a second hard gate described below.',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Work style',
    body: 'Are you genuinely comfortable with repeat-and-verify work — running the same test protocol dozens of times a day, or repeating a failed experiment until it works — rather than fast-moving, improvisational problem-solving? Diagnostic labs and research benches both run on precision and patience, not creativity under pressure.',
    note: 'If you want variety and fast-changing daily tasks, the diagnostics track especially will feel repetitive within the first year.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: "Can your family fund a private-college biomedical science seat without materially straining the household's finances, or would a state-university or affordable-college route reach the same lab and research eligibility for far less? Treat roughly 10% of your total education budget as the starting benchmark for this degree, since neither track pays enough in the first two to three years to justify a large loan.",
    note: 'Spending materially more is only justified by checkable evidence: real hospital or diagnostic-chain placement tie-ups, genuine wet-lab access, or a strong research-mentorship track record, not general college prestige.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'India\'s diagnostic-lab and clinical-research sectors are both genuinely hiring, driven by a growing organised diagnostics chain footprint and steady pharma/CRO expansion. The research track is the one exception: government scientist hiring is slow, seat-limited, and does not scale with sector growth the way private diagnostics and clinical research do.',
    note: 'Check whether your target track is inside that growth (diagnostics, clinical research, QC/QA) or outside it (pure government research), because the honest growth picture is different for each.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Differentiation',
    body: "The degree title alone does not separate you from every other biomedical science graduate applying for the same lab seat. A specific instrument certification, an NABL-accreditation understanding, a published research contribution, or a clinical-research coordinator credential does.",
    note: 'Without that added layer, you compete purely on marks and interview performance against a growing pool of graduates carrying the same base degree.',
  },
];

const verdictYes = [
  'You want a real, employable science degree that pays from year one without a research degree attached, and diagnostic or clinical-lab work genuinely interests you.',
  'You are realistic about entry pay: Rs 1.8-3.5 LPA for lab work, a fixed fellowship for research, rising only with accreditation knowledge, seniority, or a postgraduate specialisation.',
  'You are choosing biomedical science specifically for the human-biology, diagnostics, and disease angle, not because you confused it with the engineering or biotechnology degree.',
  'You are willing to add one specific proof layer on top of the base degree: an instrument or accreditation certification, a published contribution, or a clinical-research credential.',
];

const verdictNo = [
  'You are picking it as a consolation prize after missing a NEET rank for MBBS, hoping it is "medicine-adjacent" enough to feel the same.',
  'You want the research track specifically for prestige, without checking that the pay stays fixed at fellowship level for years before any scientist-grade salary is possible.',
  'You would genuinely prefer building or designing hardware over running diagnostic tests or lab experiments — that preference points to biomedical engineering, not biomedical science.',
  'You dislike repetitive, protocol-driven, accuracy-checked work, and are hoping a "science" framing will make that texture feel different than it actually will.',
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Enrolling without confirming which of the three "biomedical"-named degrees you are actually in',
    body: 'Biomedical science, biomedical engineering, and biotechnology sound alike and get mixed up on college brochures. Ask to see the actual first-year subject list before enrolling — a program that opens with electronics and circuits is not the biology-and-physiology degree this article is about.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Chasing the research track for prestige without pricing in the fellowship years',
    body: 'The JRF-to-scientist ladder is a genuinely respectable path, but treating it as a fast or well-paid one before you have actually verified current fellowship amounts and scientist-post competition is how students end up several years in, still on fixed fellowship pay, with no backup plan.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Ignoring the diagnostics track because it sounds less glamorous than "research scientist"',
    body: 'Medical laboratory science and hospital diagnostics is the fastest-paying, most consistently hiring track this degree opens, and it is the one most career talks skip in favour of pitching research or a vague "biomedical field" framing.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Is biomedical science a good career in India?',
    a: 'Yes, conditionally. It is a genuinely good choice if you want real diagnostic, lab, or clinical-research work that pays from year one without needing a research degree, and if you are realistic about entry pay (roughly Rs 1.8-3.5 LPA for lab work). It is a weaker choice if you picked it as a NEET consolation prize, if you actually want hardware and device work (that is biomedical engineering, not biomedical science), or if you want the research track purely for prestige without pricing in years of fixed fellowship pay.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is biomedical science better than biotechnology or a plain BSc in life sciences?',
    a: "For fastest realistic paying entry without a postgraduate degree, biomedical science and biotechnology are close, both open a real job (lab diagnostics for one, QC/QA for the other) that a plain BSc life sciences usually cannot match on its own, because the general degree lacks a specific applied track. Biomedical science leans specifically toward human health and diagnostics; biotechnology is broader, covering pharma, agri, and industrial applications as well. Choose based on whether your interest is specifically human diagnostics and disease, or the wider biotechnology product space.",
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the salary for a biomedical science graduate in India?',
    a: 'It depends sharply on the track. Medical lab technologists typically start around Rs 1.8-3.5 LPA, rising to Rs 3.5-6 LPA as a senior lab scientist and Rs 6-11 LPA as a lab or quality manager at an NABL-accredited facility. Clinical research and pharma QC/QA roles start in a similar band but grow on a market-negotiated curve. The pure research track runs on a fixed government fellowship, roughly Rs 37,000 a month rising to roughly Rs 42,000 a month under current UGC/DBT-linked norms, before a PhD and a separate competitive selection open scientist-grade pay.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I choose biomedical science if I missed NEET for MBBS?',
    a: 'Only if diagnostic, lab, or research work genuinely interests you on its own merits, not because it feels like the closest thing to medicine available. Biomedical science graduates support diagnosis through lab testing and research; they do not diagnose or treat patients, and that distinction matters more than it sounds once you are several years into the degree. If the appeal was specifically clinical patient care, a NEET retry, a paramedical clinical route, or a different health-adjacent path may fit better than this degree chosen as a fallback.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is there a real ownership or business path in biomedical science?',
    a: 'Yes, running or co-owning an NABL-accredited diagnostic lab, but it is not a fresh-graduate route. It typically comes after several years of lab-management experience, accreditation knowledge, and enough capital or partners to set one up. Treat it as a genuine long-term option once you have the operational experience to support it, not a shortcut out of entry-level pay.',
  },
